# Built frontend must never be resolved in upstream's favour.
#
# 2026-08-26: the Python Strategies page lost its lot-mode toggle, per-strategy
# performance panel and armed-trade gauges. Nothing had been deleted from the
# source -- the SERVED bundle was upstream's.
#
# Upstream (marketcalls/openalgo) commits its built frontend/dist to git and
# auto-builds it in CI. This fork untracked dist at 615d8d59c precisely because
# it is a build artifact. The 2.0.2.1 sync (merge 55c67c81a) then hit 36
# conflicted frontend/dist/* files and they were resolved in UPSTREAM's favour,
# documented at wiki/log.md:216-217:
#
#   "36 frontend/dist/* build artifacts -> took upstream's build, so the SPA
#    matches 2.0.2.1 without needing npm on the VPS."
#
# That is why "without needing npm" was tempting: frontend/node_modules on the
# VPS had gone stale (Jul 12) against package.json (Aug 25), which had gained
# openalgo-charts@1.6.0, so `npm run build` could not run at all. Taking
# upstream's prebuilt output looked like the cheap way out; it silently shipped
# a UI compiled from source that never contained this fork's features.
#
# `merge=ours` needs a DRIVER TO BE CONFIGURED. It is NOT built-in -- git's
# built-in low-level drivers are only text, binary and union. When git cannot
# resolve a named driver it does not warn: it silently falls back to the 3-way
# text merge and conflicts, which is exactly the situation that got resolved in
# upstream's favour last time. Verified on git 2.53.0: without the config the
# merge CONFLICTED (exit 1, conflict markers); with it, the merge was clean and
# kept ours. `union` worked with no config, proving the fallback is
# driver-resolution and not something environmental.
#
# Config cannot live in a commit, so it MUST be set in every clone:
#
#     git config merge.ours.driver true
#
# LIMITS, so nobody trusts this further than it goes:
#   - It only applies when BOTH sides track the file. If dist is untracked here
#     and tracked upstream, a merge ADDS upstream's files with no conflict, and
#     this rule never fires. Re-run `git rm -r --cached frontend/dist` after any
#     upstream sync that re-adds them.
#   - Worse: if the path is untracked AND gitignored -- the shape the VPS was in
#     -- git will SILENTLY OVERWRITE the local build with upstream's, exit 0, no
#     conflict and no warning. Git's usual refusal to clobber untracked files
#     does not apply to ignored paths.
#   - It does not rebuild anything. After an upstream sync that changes
#     package.json or frontend/src, the deploy MUST run `npm ci && npm run build`.
#     See wiki/log.md for the runbook entry.
#   - It cannot detect a stale bundle that is already committed. That is what
#     test_frontend_build_guards.py::test_referenced_bundle_contains_the_features
#     is for: it resolves index.html -> entry chunk -> page chunk and asserts the
#     SERVED artifact carries the UI, because a correct chunk sitting orphaned
#     next to the served one is precisely how this bug hid.
frontend/dist/** merge=ours
